description: Preserve RAG-ASR hotword recall delivery requirements and validation checks
alwaysApply: true
---

# Hotword Recall Delivery

- Delivery includes a recall service with a managed 100k-level hotword pool. Users manage hotwords through recall-service CRUD APIs, not by passing prompt hotwords through the old non-split path.
- The container must provide durable storage for hotword metadata and precomputed embeddings so restart/reload does not lose the prepared pool.
- Validate add/list/delete/reload behavior before claiming hotword readiness.
- Validate the end-to-end recall path: user audio or projector features -> hotword embedding retrieval -> threshold/top-k ranking -> selected hotwords injected into the split VAD-final decoder request.
- In the split architecture, prefer projector recall using the existing vLLM encoder output. Avoid starting a separate Triton encoder unless Ascend compatibility and latency are proven.
- Track hotword latency as components: queue wait, feature/projector preparation, model/adapter inference, embedding similarity search, top-k/threshold filtering, serialization, and decoder request integration.
- Hotword recall affects VAD final latency, not K2 streaming partial latency, unless the integration explicitly moves recall earlier.
- Accuracy validation must include expected hotwords appearing in final text and no obvious degradation of non-hotword transcript content.
- The ASR hotword-pool wrapper must use the same backend protocol as the running RAG-ASR service. If only the HTTP service is running, do not let `RAG_ASR_SERVICE_BACKEND=auto` fall back to Triton `/v2` calls.
- The delivery image must include RAG-ASR runtime dependencies such as `lhotse`; missing dependencies make the wrapper return 502 even when the ASR API is healthy.
- For Qwen3ASR-based hotword recall, preserve compatibility fixes for default RoPE and missing `pad_token_id` when the container's `transformers` version lacks those defaults.
